NEARS-2476 -- non-blocking observation, NOT a product defect.

During the rapid lang_first/scale_first toggle reps (6x), the app log recorded an
uncaught FlutterError several times:

  flutter: The following exception was thrown uncaught async error (FlutterError):
  flutter: [FAIL] endpoint=null http_status=null type=UncaughtAsyncError msg="uncaught async error (FlutterError)"
  flutter: The following exception was thrown uncaught async error (FlutterError):
  This RootWidget widget cannot be marked as needing to build because the framework
  is already in the process of building widgets. A widget can be marked as needing
  to be built during the build phase only if one of its ancestors is currently
  building. This exception is allowed because the framework builds parent widgets
  before children, which means a dirty descendant will always be built. Otherwise,
  the framework might not visit this widget during this build phase.

This is the EXACT artifact already documented in
docs/apps/userapp/userapp-navigation-guide.md (NEARS-2413 gotcha, "Caution when
doing this: calling Get.updateLocale/setLanguage via a raw evaluate mid-build ...
can hit GetNavigation.forceAppUpdate()'s performReassemble() from an unsafe build
phase, producing a benign-but-noisy setState() called during build uncaught [FAIL]
... this is a raw-eval-injection timing artifact, not a reachable-by-users defect;
a real language-picker tap never fires from inside another widget's build()."

It is a side effect of MY test harness firing two VM-service `evaluate` calls
back-to-back with zero settle time -- a real user cannot physically double-tap two
different Settings screens fast enough to hit this from actual UI, and no
RenderFlex/layout overflow accompanies it. Not filed as task_bugs/regression_bugs.
